 AI inference provider?", "What data they store and for how long, how customers are isolated, how fast inference engines are patched, how secrets you give them are stored, which third parties host their infrastructure, and how and when they will notify you of an incident."]], "related": ["/ai-architect/", "/fractional-cto/", "/regions/"], "citations": [{"title": "Fireworks October 2026 security incident", "url": "https://fireworks.ai/security/bulletins/october-2026-security-incident", "publisher": "Fireworks AI", "date": "Nov 2026"}, {"title": "Fireworks AI gets burned twice as customer data exposure widens", "url": "https://www.theregister.com/cyber-crime/2026/11/10/fireworks-ai-gets-burned-twice-as-customer-data-exposure-widens/5308722", "publisher": "The Register", "date": "10 Nov 2026"}, {"title": "Fireworks AI Confirms Wider Breach Affecting Customer Datasets, Model Weights and Notion AI Chats", "url": "https://thehackernews.com/2026/11/fireworks-ai-breach-widens-to-expose.html", "publisher": "The Hacker News", "date": "Nov 2026"}, {"title": "Inside the Enclave, Still Exposed: The NEAR AI SGLang Breach", "url": "https://labs.cloudsecurityalliance.org/research/csa-research-note-near-ai-sglang-confidential-inference-brea/", "publisher": "Cloud Security Alliance", "date": "Nov 2026"}, {"title": "Near AI Breach Exposes User Details and Data From 18 AI Agents", "url": "https://tech.yahoo.com/cybersecurity/articles/near-ai-breach-exposes-user-201603825.html", "publisher": "Yahoo Tech", "date": "Nov 2026"}, {"title": "Inside the LLM Call: GenAI Observability with OpenTelemetry", "url": "https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2026/genai-observability/", "publisher": "OpenTelemetry blog", "date": "14 May 2026"}]}-->
<section class="block">
<h2>What happened</h2>
<p><strong>Fireworks AI</strong> reported two October incidents. In the first, internal credentials were used to read environment variables in evaluation jobs for a limited number of customer accounts, which The Register reports included customers' API keys and tokens<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. In the second, an attacker reached Fireworks' development machines through a cloud provider hosting its research machines, accessing billing records and, for a limited set of accounts, stored secrets, datasets and incomplete model weights<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>NEAR AI</strong> disclosed that an attacker used a remote code execution flaw in the open-source SGLang inference engine to run code on one inference server, where in-flight requests, including confidential-mode ones, could be observed; API keys for two customers were exposed through logs<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What you hand an inference provider</h2>
<p>Using an inference provider feels like calling an API. In practice, you often give them much more than prompts.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Every request</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Prompts and responses<em>customer data in transit</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Often stored</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Logs<em>sometimes with keys or content</em></span><span class="hl">Secrets<em>keys for tools and other APIs</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>For fine-tuning</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Datasets<em>often your most sensitive data</em></span><span>Model weights<em>your tuned models</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Behind them</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Inference engines<em>open-source, fast-moving</em></span><span>Their cloud providers<em>a further supply chain</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Both incidents reached layers below the API: logs, stored secrets, datasets and the providers' own suppliers.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/canada-learning-app-ai-chatbot-online-safety/conversation-review.webp" alt="Aspire dashboard showing an invoke_agent trace with the user message, the assistant output, the number of tool calls, the duration and the token count" width="1464" height="982" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>A single agent call carries the user's message, the model's answer and tool activity: all of it passes through, and may be logged by, whoever serves the model. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2026/genai-observability/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry blog, James Newton-King</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Four lessons</h2>
<p><strong>1. Do not give providers long-lived keys to other systems.</strong> Both incidents involved secrets stored with the provider: environment variables in evaluation jobs and stored secrets in accounts. If an inference job needs to reach another service, give it a short-lived, narrowly scoped credential, or call that service from your side instead. See the <a href="/case-studies/south-africa-fintech-machine-agent-identity/">machine identity case study</a>.</p>
<p><strong>2. Send the minimum, keep the rest.</strong> Fine-tuning datasets uploaded months ago often stay with the provider long after the experiment ended. Delete what you no longer need, prefer providers that let you set retention, and remove personal data from training sets where you can.</p>
<p><strong>3. Confidential computing is a layer, not a guarantee.</strong> The NEAR AI case shows that a flaw in the engine inside the enclave can still expose requests<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. Ask providers how quickly they patch inference engines and what egress controls apply inside protected environments.</p>
<p><strong>4. Your provider has providers.</strong> The second Fireworks intrusion came through a cloud provider hosting research machines<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Ask which third parties host the provider's infrastructure, including research and development environments, not just production.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Questions to ask before trusting a provider with data</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Ask</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>What do you store from my requests, and for how long?</td><td>Logs and caches outlive the request</td></tr>
<tr><td>How are customers isolated from each other?</td><td>One compromised account should not expose others</td></tr>
<tr><td>How fast do you patch inference engines?</td><td>Engine flaws were the entry point at NEAR AI</td></tr>
<tr><td>How are secrets I give you stored and who can read them?</td><td>Stored secrets were exposed at Fireworks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Which third parties host any of your environments?</td><td>Development environments were the route in</td></tr>
<tr><td>How and when will you notify me of an incident?</td><td>You need time to rotate keys and assess impact</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can I delete datasets and fine-tuned weights on demand?</td><td>What is gone cannot leak</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="callout"><p>An inference provider is a data processor holding some of your most sensitive information. Choose and manage it the way you would a payments processor, not a developer tool.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>If you use an affected provider</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li class="hl"><b>Rotate</b><span>Rotate any keys or tokens stored with the provider</span></li>
<li><b>Review</b><span>Check activity on services those keys could reach</span></li>
<li><b>Inventory</b><span>List datasets and weights you have uploaded</span></li>
<li><b>Delete</b><span>Remove what you no longer need</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Prepare</b><span>Make sure traffic can move to another provider</span></li>
<li><b>Record</b><span>Document your assessment for customers and regulators</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>A short checklist for this week, whichever provider you use.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>CTO take</h2>
<p>AI providers have grown faster than their security, and attackers have noticed: LLMjacking gangs want keys, and fine-tuning datasets are valuable in their own right. The answer is not to stop using providers, but to treat them as critical suppliers: minimise what you send, avoid leaving secrets with them, demand clear answers on patching and third parties, and keep the ability to move. Read alongside <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-11-12/">the 12 November tech news</a>, <a href="/insights/llmjacking-stolen-ai-keys-black-october/">LLMjacking and stolen AI keys</a> and the <a href="/case-studies/korea-fintech-self-hosted-inference-security/">self-hosted inference security case study</a>.</p>
</section>

